BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:14ed7f9bba4d544d8980b0a27e0da0fa6a303581@pushpin.city
DTSTAMP:20260919T013100Z
DTSTART:20260922T210000Z
DTEND:20260922T230000Z
SUMMARY:John Gallagher: “Students still think they write better than the 
 machines: A study of undergrad self-perceptions of Generative AI and writi
 ng skills”
LOCATION:MIT\, 32 VASSAR ST\, Cambridge\, MA 02139
DESCRIPTION:Since ChatGPT arrived\, surveys suggest that 86% of students us
 e GenAI in their academic work. But what do students really think about th
 ese tools\, and about their own writing? In this talk\, I share findings f
 rom a large-scale survey (n=342) of University of Illinois undergraduates 
 conducted in spring 2026. These students occupy a unique moment in history
 : they learned to write before GenAI existed\, which makes them well posit
 ioned to compare their own abilities to a machine’s. The results may sur
 prise you. Students report high familiarity with AI but lukewarm trust. Ne
 arly half of humanities students say they never use these tools at all\, w
 hile business and STEM students use and trust them the most. When asked to
  compare their writing to GenAI\, students overwhelmingly judged their own
  work superior\, insisting that their ideas are better. Along the way\, sp
 eaker John Gallagher discusses research that shows GenAI erodes writing ab
 ility over time by making us less persistent. Whether you use AI every day
 \, avoid it entirely\, or simply wonder what it means for how we write and
  think\, this conversation is for you.\nhttps://calendar.mit.edu/event/joh
 n-gallagher-undergrad-self-perceptions-generative-ai-writing-skills\nFrom 
 Pushpin Boston: https://pushpin.city/boston/
URL:https://calendar.mit.edu/event/john-gallagher-undergrad-self-perception
 s-generative-ai-writing-skills
END:VEVENT
